## BrightPath Community Grants Policy 2025-2026

### 7. Eligibility Criteria

7.1 **Applicant Organisations.** To be eligible for a BrightPath Community Grant, an applicant organisation must, at the time of application and for the duration of the grant period, satisfy all of the following criteria:

(a) be registered as a charity, a society, a company limited by guarantee, or a similar not-for-profit entity under the laws of Singapore;

(b) have been in operation for at least 12 months prior to the application closing date. For the 2025-2026 cycle, the closing date is 30 September 2025. Organisations that have been in operation for less than 12 months may apply only if they are able to demonstrate that they are operating under the auspices of an eligible umbrella organisation, which must provide a letter of support;

(c) have a physical presence in Singapore, such as a registered office, a community space, or a programme venue. A postal address alone will not satisfy this criterion;

(d) have a valid Unique Entity Number (UEN) issued by the Accounting and Corporate Regulatory Authority (ACRA) or the Registry of Societies (ROS);

(e) have a bank account in the name of the organisation, held with a bank licensed in Singapore;

(f) not be an excluded entity as described in section 7.4 below; and

(g) be able to demonstrate that the proposed project will be delivered in Singapore and will benefit residents of Singapore.

7.2 **Umbrella Organisations.** Where an applicant organisation is applying under the auspices of an umbrella organisation, the umbrella organisation must itself satisfy the eligibility criteria in section 7.1 (other than the 12-month operating requirement). The umbrella organisation will be the signatory to the grant agreement and will be responsible for the proper use of grant funds.

7.3 **Ineligible Organisations.** The following organisations are not eligible to apply:

(a) government agencies, statutory boards, and organs of state;

(b) political parties and organisations whose primary purpose is political advocacy;

(c) religious organisations, unless the proposed project is a community service project that is open to persons of all faiths and does not include any element of proselytisation;

(d) schools, unless the proposed project is a community project that is not primarily for the benefit of the school's own students;

(e) commercial entities, including social enterprises that are structured as commercial companies and that distribute profits to shareholders;

(f) individuals applying in their personal capacity; and

(g) organisations that are, at the time of application, subject to a suspension or debarment by any Singapore government agency, or that have outstanding audit queries or unresolved financial reports in respect of any prior grant from a Singapore government agency.

7.4 **Excluded Entities.** An organisation will be treated as an excluded entity for the purposes of this Policy if:

(a) it has been convicted of any offence involving fraud, dishonesty, or the misuse of public funds within the five years preceding the application closing date;

(b) it has been found by a court or tribunal to have breached any law relating to the protection of personal data, within the three years preceding the application closing date;

(c) it is insolvent, is in liquidation, or has a receiver or judicial manager appointed over its assets; or

(d) it has had a grant from the BrightPath Community Grants programme terminated for cause within the two years preceding the application closing date.
**BrightPath Community Grants Policy 2025-2026**

**Part 8: Project Reporting and Conditions**

8.1 **Reporting Obligations.** A grantee must submit to the Grants Operations Office the following reports in respect of a funded project:

(a) an interim progress report, due no later than the date stated in the grant agreement, which shall describe the activities carried out to date, the outputs achieved against the milestones in the approved work plan, and any significant deviation from the work plan;

(b) a final project report, due within 60 days after the project completion date stated in the grant agreement, which shall describe the project outcomes, the outputs delivered, the number of beneficiaries served, and lessons learned; and

(c) a financial report, due together with the final project report, which shall set out the actual expenditure of grant funds against the approved budget, with supporting receipts and other documents as specified in the grant agreement.

8.2 **Report Format.** All reports must be submitted in the format prescribed by the Grants Operations Office from time to time. Reports may be required to be submitted through the online grant management system. Where a grantee is unable to submit a report electronically, the grantee may request, in writing, permission to submit the report in hard copy.

8.3 **Certification by Authorised Signatory.** Each report must be certified by the authorised signatory of the grantee organisation, or by the umbrella organisation's authorised signatory where the grant is held by an umbrella organisation under section 7.2. The certification shall confirm that the information in the report is true, accurate, and complete, and that the grant funds were used only for the purposes of the project.

8.4 **Site Visits and Inspections.** The Grants Operations Office, or any person authorised by it, may carry out site visits, inspections, and audits of a grantee's premises, records, and project activities. The grantee must provide all reasonable access and assistance for this purpose. Site visits may be announced or unannounced.

8.5 **Records to be Kept.** A grantee must keep, for a period of at least five years from the date of the final report, full and proper records relating to the project, including:

(a) financial records, including invoices, receipts, bank statements, and payroll records;

(b) records of project activities, including attendance lists, photographs, and publicity materials;

(c) correspondence with the Grants Operations Office; and

(d) any other records that the Grants Operations Office may reasonably require.

8.6 **Acknowledgment of Support.** A grantee must acknowledge the support of the BrightPath Community Grants programme in all publicity materials, publications, and other outputs arising from the funded project. The form of acknowledgment will be specified in the grant agreement. A grantee must not use the BrightPath name or logo in any way that suggests endorsement of the grantee organisation or its activities beyond the funded project.

8.7 **Conditions on the Use of Funds.** Grant funds must be used only for the approved project and for the budget items set out in the approved budget. A grantee must not, without the prior written approval of the Grants Operations Office:

(a) transfer grant funds between budget lines where the transfer exceeds 10% of the total grant amount;

(b) use grant funds for any capital expenditure exceeding $5,000 per item;

